<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Birger Jarl</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1210–<a href="/years/1266">1266</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Birger Magnusson</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Birger Jarl was a Swedish statesman who held the office of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jarl">jarl</a> from 1248 and governed for his son King Valdemar, founding the Folkung dynasty. He strengthened royal law and authority, is traditionally credited with founding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stockholm">Stockholm</a> around 1250, and led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> that brought Tavastia in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> under Swedish rule in the mid-13th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/birger-jarl.jpg" alt="Birger Jarl" width="255" height="353" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Axel Forssén (1888-1961) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Birger_jarl_(Forss%C3%A9n).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Birger Magnusson belonged to the House of Bjälbo and grew up at Bjälbo in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostergotland">Östergötland</a>. His father was Magnus Minnesköld, and his mother Ingrid Ylva was, according to Olaus Petri, a daughter of Sune Sik and granddaughter of King Sverker I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>. In the mid-1230s, after competition with other suitors, he married Ingeborg Eriksdotter, the sister of King Erik Eriksson, as Erik's Chronicle tells. The marriage gave him his base of power, and he was probably among the most influential men in the kingdom for years before King Erik formally gave him the title of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jarl">jarl</a> in 1248.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>According to Erik's Chronicle, Birger led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-swedish-crusade">Second Swedish Crusade</a> across the Baltic to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a>, where he defeated the pagan Tavastians and forced them to convert. The expedition is traditionally dated to 1249–1250, though many scholars now place it in 1238–1239, before he was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jarl">jarl</a>. It extended Swedish rule inland, and the Swedes began building a castle in Tavastia.</p><p>Russian sources also name Birger as the leader of a campaign in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ingria">Ingria</a> in 1240 that ended in defeat by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-nevsky">Alexander Nevsky</a> at the Battle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neva-river">Neva</a>. Swedish sources do not mention the battle, and the first sources linking Birger to it date from the mid-15th century.</p><p>Around 1248 the papal diplomat <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-of-modena">William of Modena</a> urged the Swedish kings to follow the rules of the Church, and Birger took the Church's side against other members of his family. In 1249 Birger ended decades of hostility with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> by the Treaty of Lödöse, marrying his eleven-year-old daughter Rikissa to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haakon-iv-of-norway">Haakon Haakonsson</a> the Young. When King Erik died in 1250, Birger's son Valdemar was elected king, and Birger ruled as regent. In 1252 two letters of his gave the first mention of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stockholm">Stockholm</a>, which tradition treats as its foundation, though what stood there earlier is debated.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He died on 21 October 1266 at Jälbolung in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vastergotland">Västergötland</a> and was buried at Varnhem Abbey, where his grave was forgotten after a fire in the 16th century and rediscovered in the 1920s. He was the last Swedish jarl, since the title was replaced by duke after his death. The historian Thomas Lindkvist ranks him and Birger Brosa as the most powerful men of their eras in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>. His sons Valdemar and Magnus Ladulås both became kings. A statue of him by Bengt Erland Fogelberg, raised in 1854, stands in Birger Jarls Torg next to Riddarholm Church in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stockholm">Stockholm</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Birger Jarl” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Birger Jarl</b>, who governed for his son King Valdemar from 1250 until 1266, strengthened the crown, issued peace laws, founded Stockholm, first mentioned in 1252, and led an expedition, traditionally dated to 1249, that brought Tavastia in Finland under Swedish rule.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-sweden">Kingdom of Sweden · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>A Swedish expedition led by <b>Birger Jarl</b>, known as the Second Swedish Crusade and traditionally dated 1249, subdued Tavastia and built Häme Castle.</blockquote><a href="/state/tavastia">Tavastia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Bjälbo, in what is now <a href="/countries/sweden">Sweden</a>. Died in Jälbolung. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=58.37,15&amp;year=1246" aria-label="Bjälbo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4869 194 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Birger Jarl"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5319 464h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5319 464h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=58.37,15&amp;year=1246">What was here in 1246? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
